1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,750
On the seventh of April, Django
four-point-two reached end of life.

2
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:06,550
No more security fixes.

3
00:00:07,583 --> 00:00:12,033
But Debian thirteen and Ubuntu
twenty-four-oh-four still ship it.

4
00:00:12,067 --> 00:00:15,767
Since then, Django has published
nineteen security advisories

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,200
— and not one official
patch for four-point-two.

6
00:00:20,233 --> 00:00:24,233
On October first, Ubuntu shipped
a backport for one of them.

7
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:28,333
Five days later, Django said
its original fix was incomplete.

8
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:30,467
We took Ubuntu's source package,

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:33,917
ran a test calibrated on
Django's own fix commits…

10
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:38,483
and the code path Django closed is
still reachable in Ubuntu's build.

11
00:00:39,517 --> 00:00:41,183
That test was run by Heirloom

12
00:00:41,217 --> 00:00:44,400
— an agent that keeps
end-of-life software patched,

13
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:48,033
and proves which old versions
are actually vulnerable.

14
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:53,050
Its first rule: never trust
a test that hasn't failed.

15
00:00:53,083 --> 00:00:56,433
For each advisory,
Heirloom writes its own test,

16
00:00:56,467 --> 00:01:01,233
proves it fails on Django's
vulnerable code and passes on the fix…

17
00:01:01,267 --> 00:01:06,033
and only then asks the real question:
is the old version affected?

18
00:01:07,067 --> 00:01:10,700
Then we let it run the whole
campaign: nineteen advisories,

19
00:01:10,733 --> 00:01:13,733
replayed in the order
Django published them.

20
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:17,650
Thirteen candidate backports
— each one failing before the fix,

21
00:01:17,683 --> 00:01:19,167
and passing after.

22
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,117
Where it couldn't prove something,
it filed the evidence instead of guessing.

23
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:27,683
And when a new fix amended
one it had already backported,

24
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:31,000
it stacked the follow-up
on its own pull request.

25
00:01:32,033 --> 00:01:35,167
Across the whole campaign,
it asked me one question

26
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:39,283
— a real judgement call about
changing Django's behaviour.

27
00:01:39,317 --> 00:01:40,767
I answered once.

28
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:43,267
It sealed the answer as a precedent,

29
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:46,433
and the next case reused
it without asking.

30
00:01:47,467 --> 00:01:50,800
We tested the method on
cases with known answers.

31
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:54,500
A naive pipeline once called
a vulnerable version safe.

32
00:01:54,533 --> 00:01:56,217
Heirloom: zero times.

33
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:00,167
Every claim in every pull request
links to its neat-logs trace,

34
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,817
and every backport links the Entire
session of the agent that wrote it.

35
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:09,317
Each advisory cost about
twenty cents of model time.

36
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:12,000
We modelled the business in C-F-O dot A-I:

37
00:02:12,033 --> 00:02:15,917
what that's worth to the teams
who keep old software alive.

38
00:02:16,950 --> 00:02:17,933
Heirloom.

39
00:02:17,967 --> 00:02:19,983
Old software, kept honest.

40
00:02:20,017 --> 00:02:22,917
Every verdict is a test run you can open.
